goal here is to offer a small set of

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:23.760 align:start position:19%
policy principles and design patterns
that can help New Mexico make those

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:30.320 align:start position:19%
decisions safely. The core idea is that
this trust by design uh and this is not

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:35.200 align:start position:19%
primarily a technology problem. It is a
trust and governance problem that

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:40.080 align:start position:19%
technology then has to implement. Now
I'm approaching this from a systems

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:44.480 align:start position:19%
cloud security uh data governance
perspective.

00:09:41.519 --> 00:09:49.519 align:start position:19%
uh the gory details can come later but
today I want to focus on the questions

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:56.320 align:start position:19%
that need to be asked before those
details are built.

00:09:53.040 --> 00:10:02.720 align:start position:19%
So moving on here I I I really want to
make clear before we get into um some

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:10.080 align:start position:19%
more of the technical stuff
the human stakes why data trust matters.

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:13.600 align:start position:19%
Privacy here is not just a compliance
requirement. Patient trust can affect

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:18.880 align:start position:19%
whether people are willing to
participate in the program at all. As we

00:10:16.959 --> 00:10:22.800 align:start position:19%
all know, you know, psychedelic care may
involve some of the most intimate

00:10:20.399 --> 00:10:27.360 align:start position:19%
information a person ever shares.
trauma, mental health history, substance

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:33.040 align:start position:19%
use, you know, deeply personal
experiences during altered states,

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:37.120 align:start position:19%
sometimes recording, sensor data, and
you know, yes, breaches can cause

00:10:34.959 --> 00:10:41.120 align:start position:19%
lawsuits, reputational damage,
regulatory consequences, but I don't

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:45.440 align:start position:19%
want to spend our limited time on
generic breach scenarios. The more

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:49.920 align:start position:19%
important point for this program is
upstream. If people fear that seeking

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:54.720 align:start position:19%
treatment will create a permanent
commercially exploitable or government

00:10:52.399 --> 00:11:00.640 align:start position:19%
accessible record of those experiences,
some people will simply avoid treatment.

00:10:57.839 --> 00:11:08.000 align:start position:19%
That's why I'm treating trust as part of
the safety and accessibility model, not

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:14.120 align:start position:19%
as a public relations concern.
Now the data landscape, today's data and

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:14.120 align:start position:19%
tomorrow's

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:21.120 align:start position:19%
uh the rules that we write now can't
really assume that medical data will

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:27.040 align:start position:19%
continue to look like rows and columns
in electronic health record. As we all

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:31.760 align:start position:19%
know things move so quickly. Today we
have familiar clinical data. Um,

00:11:29.839 --> 00:11:36.079 align:start position:19%
psychedelic care also creates rich
experiential data like free form

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:41.360 align:start position:19%
narrative, facilitator observations,
audio, video and you know the frontier

00:11:39.519 --> 00:11:46.000 align:start position:19%
is already moving toward wearables,
biometrics, eye tracking, motion,

00:11:44.399 --> 00:11:52.320 align:start position:19%
neurohysiology,
immersive headset telemetry, AI derived

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:58.800 align:start position:19%
inferences etc etc. Now some of the
standards that are already written by

00:11:56.240 --> 00:12:03.040 align:start position:19%
federal agencies or NIST or or things
like that uh things have have already

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:09.519 align:start position:19%
progressed
um past those. And so

00:12:06.959 --> 00:12:13.760 align:start position:19%
I you know I want to make an important
distinction. Uh I'd like to talk about

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:17.920 align:start position:19%
data minimization but I'd like to make
an important distinction about it. I'm

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:21.920 align:start position:19%
not arguing that every provider should
collect as little data as possible.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:27.680 align:start position:19%
There may be very good reasons with
meaningful patient consent obviously to

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:32.160 align:start position:19%
capture very rich multimodal data.
Future therapies may use many signals

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:37.040 align:start position:19%
from a piece experience for instance to
help patients revisit or integrate

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:41.120 align:start position:19%
aspects of that experience later.
Data minization is important but I think

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:47.680 align:start position:19%
the better principle
is collect intentionally

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:53.279 align:start position:19%
expose minimally and use purposefully.
Maximum useful collection doesn't

00:12:50.079 --> 00:12:58.000 align:start position:19%
require maximum exposure.
Policy should separately govern

00:12:55.200 --> 00:13:02.079 align:start position:19%
collection, retention, access, and
disclosure. [clears throat] And it

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:07.600 align:start position:19%
should regulate categories of risk and
use rather than hard- coding a list of

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:10.600 align:start position:19%
today's devices or even today's data
fields.

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:18.240 align:start position:19%
Now, let's move on to deidentification.
I'm kind of presenting a working

00:13:15.839 --> 00:13:22.880 align:start position:19%
vocabulary here. Um, but
deidentification is not a binary thing.

00:13:20.959 --> 00:13:28.160 align:start position:19%
It's not that data is either identified
or deidentified and that's it. There's

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:33.360 align:start position:19%
somewhat of a spectrum. And I I want to
make clear that these are my working

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:38.079 align:start position:19%
definitions for this talk. Legal and
technical definitions vary by context.

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:42.639 align:start position:19%
And obviously New Mexico will be will
will want to write their own clear

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:48.959 align:start position:19%
definitions of these things. But for the
today's talk, identified data is

00:13:45.839 --> 00:13:54.959 align:start position:19%
directly linked to a person. When we say
pseudonmized uh interesting little word

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:59.920 align:start position:19%
there um or also what we call relinkable
data means that direct identifiers have

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:04.720 align:start position:19%
been replaced or separated but an
authorized linkage mechanism still

00:14:02.079 --> 00:14:08.720 align:start position:19%
exists to reidentify that data.
Deidentified data means that the

00:14:07.279 --> 00:14:14.880 align:start position:19%
identification risk has been
deliberately reduced to an agreed level,

00:14:11.199 --> 00:14:21.360 align:start position:19%
but it's not automatically safe. Uh risk
is not necessarily zero. Um there are

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:24.959 align:start position:19%
multiple ways to to relink that data.
There's things called linkage attacks

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:29.600 align:start position:19%
and so on. We won't get too far into
that today. Then aggregated data, I

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:34.399 align:start position:19%
think everyone knows what that means.
That's information that's reported at a

00:14:31.519 --> 00:14:39.199 align:start position:19%
group level. uh but small sets of
aggregated data, small cells can still

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:44.000 align:start position:19%
create identification risk. And then at
the very end we have anonymized data

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:49.279 align:start position:19%
which for this presentation means that
no reasonable mechanism remains to

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:53.600 align:start position:19%
relink that information to a person. Now
risk remains on risk depends on what

00:14:51.760 --> 00:15:00.320 align:start position:19%
remains in the data and what it can be
linked to. The identification is risk

00:14:56.800 --> 00:15:05.199 align:start position:19%
management and not a magic switch. These
terms are often used kind of

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:09.600 align:start position:19%
inconsistently. Um but I you know I want
to just emphasize that a gooid or a

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:14.959 align:start position:19%
globally unique identifier something
random that that is linked to uh patient

00:15:12.079 --> 00:15:20.240 align:start position:19%
data for instance doesn't must make that
data anonymous if another system can

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:25.839 align:start position:19%
resolve that gooid back to a person. The
key point again is that risk depends on

00:15:22.959 --> 00:15:29.519 align:start position:19%
what remains. Dates, locations, rare
attributes, narrative, highdimensional

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:33.839 align:start position:19%
signals, and and what outside
information can be linked to it. A data

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:41.360 align:start position:19%
set that is difficult to reidentify
today may be easier to reidentify in the

00:15:36.959 --> 00:15:45.279 align:start position:19%
future. And I have a a fuller glossery
in the backup slides that, you know,

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:50.959 align:start position:19%
I'll share this slide at the end or this
deck at the end here.

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:56.560 align:start position:19%
Now, this is really important to me and
I feel like it's very relevant to New

00:15:53.120 --> 00:16:01.519 align:start position:19%
Mexico. You don't need a name to know
who somebody is. They could not have a

00:15:59.839 --> 00:16:05.279 align:start position:19%
name in their data, not have a social
security number, and still be

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:10.880 align:start position:19%
identifiable.
We call some of these, sometimes we call

00:16:08.079 --> 00:16:14.240 align:start position:19%
these quasi identifiers. Removing names
and numbers isn't really enough.

00:16:12.399 --> 00:16:19.360 align:start position:19%
Ordinary attributes can become
identifying when combined. In New

00:16:16.959 --> 00:16:23.360 align:start position:19%
Mexico, I think this deserves particular
attention because some rural, tribal,

00:16:21.759 --> 00:16:29.680 align:start position:19%
and other communities are small enough
that combinations of age, affiliation,

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:34.720 align:start position:19%
location, diagnosis, and did that person
have treatment on Tuesday or maybe a

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:39.440 align:start position:19%
rare event uh may point to one single
person and everybody could know who that

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:45.360 align:start position:19%
person is even without having
traditional uh identifiable data.

00:16:43.199 --> 00:16:51.600 align:start position:19%
So I would say that we want to treat
this respectfully as a privacy risk

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:57.120 align:start position:19%
example. Definitely not here as not
bringing this as an attempt to prescribe

00:16:53.759 --> 00:17:06.160 align:start position:19%
tribal level data sovereignity policy.
But um you know I I think that that um

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:10.959 align:start position:19%
it's difficult to identify what all the
quasi identifiers are. I kind of

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:14.480 align:start position:19%
anticipate I have had the question many
times like how do I identify all the

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:19.199 align:start position:19%
quasi identifiers and and the answer
honestly is that we really can't solve

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:24.240 align:start position:19%
this with a static list. Identifiability
depends on combinations of attributes,

00:17:21.199 --> 00:17:28.000 align:start position:19%
granularity, population size, what
external information is available.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:33.520 align:start position:19%
That's why deidentification should
include disclosure, risk assessment, and

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:38.720 align:start position:19%
reidentification testing, not simply
just removal of fields that we label PII

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:43.200 align:start position:19%
or personally identifiable information.
And you know from a data security

00:17:40.640 --> 00:17:49.280 align:start position:19%
standpoint we we want to think like an
attacker sometimes but in privacy terms

00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:53.760 align:start position:19%
we say you know given this proposed
release and reasonably available outside

00:17:51.520 --> 00:18:00.160 align:start position:19%
information how successfully could
someone figure out who these people are.

00:17:56.080 --> 00:18:03.440 align:start position:19%
Now there are some um for instance NIST
National Institute of Standards and

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:07.360 align:start position:19%
Technology special publications that I
that I'll reference in another slide

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:10.880 align:start position:19%
that I'll share later that deal uh
specifically with this kind of stuff and

00:18:09.039 --> 00:18:19.760 align:start position:19%
there's there's studies and guidance on
this um that New Mexico can refer to.

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:24.880 align:start position:19%
So security, privacy and governments are
different problems.

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:30.000 align:start position:19%
So just a little vocabulary here.
Security. When we talk about security,

00:18:26.799 --> 00:18:37.760 align:start position:19%
we're asking who can get to the data.
Privacy asks what can that data reveal

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:41.520 align:start position:19%
about a person. Data governance asks why
we have the data, who's allowed to use

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:46.799 align:start position:19%
it, and what they are allowed to do with
it. Now, a system can be extraordinarily

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:51.200 align:start position:19%
secure. We can have encryption and
identity management and logging and

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:56.000 align:start position:19%
backups and all the things, but we could
still have a terrible privacy policy if

00:18:53.760 --> 00:19:03.360 align:start position:19%
that organization is permitted to use
patient information for purposes that

00:18:58.559 --> 00:19:08.000 align:start position:19%
patients never reasonably expected.
Now, [clears throat]

00:19:04.240 --> 00:19:12.160 align:start position:19%
just to touch on sock and ISO and and
compliance standards,

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:16.720 align:start position:19%
I think those can be useful evidence of
an of organizational maturity when we're

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:21.919 align:start position:19%
talking about, for instance, vendors,
providers in the space, um, that sort of

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:26.640 align:start position:19%
thing. But these are baselines. They
don't answer New Mexico's core policy

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:30.480 align:start position:19%
question which is what an organization
should be permitted to do with

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:39.200 align:start position:19%
psychedelic patient data. Compliance
with SOCK 2 and ISO 27701 and etc is a

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:44.320 align:start position:19%
floor but trust is the goal.
Now

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:50.080 align:start position:19%
reidentification of data must be
exceptional and governed.

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:54.640 align:start position:19%
Here this diagram is just an
illustrative control pattern. It's it's

00:19:52.320 --> 00:20:00.000 align:start position:19%
I'm not suggesting New Mexico mandate a
particular database architecture. Uh the

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:07.200 align:start position:19%
important concepts here are separation
of identity from sensitive data, least

00:20:03.760 --> 00:20:14.160 align:start position:19%
privilege access policies, separation of
duties, what we call controlled linkage

00:20:10.559 --> 00:20:20.080 align:start position:19%
and very importantly auditability. Now
I'm just giving a pattern here and this

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:24.720 align:start position:19%
is a possible pattern. Um is an identity
vault separated from clinical or

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:30.480 align:start position:19%
research data that has a dedicated
linkage service in between ordinary

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:34.720 align:start position:19%
users almost any nobody should be able
to traverse this entire chain by

00:20:32.559 --> 00:20:39.760 align:start position:19%
themselves. Now for sufficiently
sensitive reidentification New Mexico

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:43.360 align:start position:19%
could consider dual authorization what
we call the two key pattern kind of like

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:47.120 align:start position:19%
a nuclear launch. you know both people
have to turn the keys but what that

00:20:45.039 --> 00:20:51.440 align:start position:19%
means in this context is that one person
requests and another independently

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:57.120 align:start position:19%
approves. This is an established
security control concept. Now there's

00:20:54.640 --> 00:21:00.799 align:start position:19%
some possible legit legitimate
categories and obviously there could be

00:20:59.039 --> 00:21:05.760 align:start position:19%
more but you know for the purposes of
this talk I thinking about patient

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:10.799 align:start position:19%
directed reidentification possibly for
access to their data correction of data

00:21:08.720 --> 00:21:16.159 align:start position:19%
uh authorized disclosure maybe to other
providers so on um and appropriate

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:20.640 align:start position:19%
deletion or data withdrawal requests.
Now we obviously have safety and

00:21:18.320 --> 00:21:25.360 align:start position:19%
continuity of care
um which you know could be adverse

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:31.280 align:start position:19%
events, adverse events, emergency care
coordination or even like product or lot

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:37.360 align:start position:19%
recall if something's um you know wrong
with a certain lot of product. Obviously

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:43.520 align:start position:19%
there's also legal and regulatory um
concerns. there's valid legal compulsion

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:48.720 align:start position:19%
like a subpoena or legal court order um
or narrowly defined oversight.

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:53.840 align:start position:19%
And
I'll also throw in that there could be

00:21:51.679 --> 00:21:59.440 align:start position:19%
what I call exceptional authorized
research only when identity is genuinely

00:21:56.559 --> 00:22:05.200 align:start position:19%
necessary and appropriate oversight
exists. Now every event, every

00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:10.080 align:start position:19%
reidentification event should be logged
and auditable. Routine patientdirected

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:15.679 align:start position:19%
events like deletion requests might be
aggregated for oversight. Exceptional

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:22.880 align:start position:19%
reidentification may merit individual
reporting or prior approval. Now, in an

00:22:19.919 --> 00:22:27.760 align:start position:19%
emergency scenario, we could have a
controlled quote unquote break glass

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:34.919 align:start position:19%
path that might be appropriate, but
breaking that glass should always create

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:34.919 align:start position:19%
an unavoidable audit event.

00:22:35.039 --> 00:22:46.559 align:start position:19%
Now this is the ethical core of my
little talk today and

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:52.480 align:start position:19%
that is that patient data should serve
the patient at at the end of the day.

00:22:49.760 --> 00:22:57.520 align:start position:19%
That's the bottom line. And I think you
know we've all had experiences with

00:22:55.520 --> 00:23:03.600 align:start position:19%
concern being concerned about our data
being sold or used in in ways that we

00:23:00.799 --> 00:23:08.720 align:start position:19%
don't expect or don't want.
You know, this is broader than just

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:12.000 align:start position:19%
simply saying data cannot be sold. Uh,
you know, a sophisticated business can

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:16.559 align:start position:19%
monetize information without calling
that transaction a sale.

00:23:14.559 --> 00:23:20.640 align:start position:19%
Uh, they could, you know, licensing,
data brokerage, profile enrichment, what

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:26.240 align:start position:19%
they what they call that, targeted
advertising, so on and so forth. Um, you

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:32.559 align:start position:19%
know, or packaging supposedly anonymous
insights from inadequately deidentified

00:23:29.200 --> 00:23:36.320 align:start position:19%
records can can pose a risk. So
companies should absolutely be able to

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:42.320 align:start position:19%
build successful businesses delivering
treatment um analytics, software,

00:23:39.760 --> 00:23:46.400 align:start position:19%
infrastructure, responsible research
services. But the distinction here is

00:23:44.559 --> 00:23:54.080 align:start position:19%
that the patients intimate information
itself should never become the product.

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:58.400 align:start position:19%
Purpose limitation is critical.
Information collected for care should

00:23:56.000 --> 00:24:03.440 align:start position:19%
never silently become marketing
unrelated product development or like

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:09.919 align:start position:19%
general purpose AI training data.
Optional secondary uses should be

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:14.000 align:start position:19%
genuinely separable where practical. Now
transparency also and I feel like this

00:24:11.919 --> 00:24:18.720 align:start position:19%
is important to all of us and we all can
relate to this. Transparency should be

00:24:15.919 --> 00:24:24.559 align:start position:19%
human readable. A patient should be able
to answer what are you collecting?

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:28.960 align:start position:19%
Um, why are you collecting it? Who can
see it? Who receives it downstream? How

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:34.559 align:start position:19%
long is it kept? Can it be used for
research or AI? How do I get a copy of

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:42.320 align:start position:19%
my own data? How do I request it to be
deleted? When can it be rel to me? Now,

00:24:38.559 --> 00:24:46.720 align:start position:19%
having a legal privacy policy may still
be a good thing, but I think that the

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:52.559 align:start position:19%
patients should also be receiving a
concise plain language data rights

00:24:49.279 --> 00:24:57.200 align:start position:19%
notice or even a little explainer video.
Um, a privacy right really isn't

00:24:55.120 --> 00:25:02.587 align:start position:19%
meaningful if exercising your privacy
right requires you to become a a privacy

00:24:59.279 --> 00:25:06.159 align:start position:19%
law expert.
&gt;&gt; [clears throat]

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:13.840 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; Now I consider myself a scientist at
heart and I love research and I want to

00:25:09.679 --> 00:25:19.679 align:start position:19%
absolutely enable research in every way
but I want to do that without exposing

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:24.480 align:start position:19%
people. Um the goal isn't to lock data
away. The goal is to match the

00:25:21.440 --> 00:25:28.960 align:start position:19%
disclosure to the question. And I put
this in kind of a latter format. What I

00:25:26.559 --> 00:25:33.840 align:start position:19%
call progressive disclosure. We start
with the least identifying form of data

00:25:31.600 --> 00:25:38.559 align:start position:19%
capable of answering the legitimate
question, often a research question. If

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:42.000 align:start position:19%
you need aggregate data, you give the
aggregate. If you need demographic

00:25:40.159 --> 00:25:46.320 align:start position:19%
trends, you give only the demographic
detail that's actually needed. You need

00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:51.520 align:start position:19%
detailed longitudinal
analysis, maybe consider a protected

00:25:49.760 --> 00:25:56.159 align:start position:19%
research environment or maybe a
controlled query model rather than

00:25:53.679 --> 00:26:02.240 align:start position:19%
distributing raw copies all over the
place. Um and then you know if

00:25:59.520 --> 00:26:05.679 align:start position:19%
identifiable data is needed which it
could be in certain cases but that

00:26:04.159 --> 00:26:09.919 align:start position:19%
should require extraordinary
justification.

00:26:07.360 --> 00:26:14.159 align:start position:19%
Now this becomes especially important in
small populations like we talked about

00:26:11.760 --> 00:26:20.559 align:start position:19%
earlier where demographic detail that
feels harmless can become identifying.

00:26:17.600 --> 00:26:25.200 align:start position:19%
Uh again referring to NIST special
publication 800188. They discuss

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:30.159 align:start position:19%
multiple data sharing models including
deidentified releases, synthetic data,

00:26:28.159 --> 00:26:35.440 align:start position:19%
query interfaces, protected data
enclaves as well as disclosure review

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:40.400 align:start position:19%
boards, reidentification studies. Now I
think research researchers should divi

00:26:38.080 --> 00:26:45.679 align:start position:19%
derive an enormous value from this
program and will do so. But in most

00:26:43.279 --> 00:26:53.919 align:start position:19%
cases, researchers simply don't need to
know who that participant is.

00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:59.200 align:start position:19%
So I think these are some of the things
that New Mexico should define. Now, now

00:26:56.159 --> 00:27:03.919 align:start position:19%
this is intentionally a set of
questions. It's not a comprehensive rule

00:27:01.039 --> 00:27:08.240 align:start position:19%
book. RECI doesn't need to build the
system today, but it needs to define the

00:27:06.080 --> 00:27:12.480 align:start position:19%
policy boundaries clearly enough that
providers, vendors, researchers,

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:16.320 align:start position:19%
engineers, etc. can build within them
safely.

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:21.600 align:start position:19%
Now, the questions here include, you
know, what purposes justify collecting

00:27:18.159 --> 00:27:26.880 align:start position:19%
and using patient data? What uses should
be prohibited outright?

00:27:24.240 --> 00:27:30.159 align:start position:19%
What's the minimum information that
should cross each organizational

00:27:28.559 --> 00:27:36.159 align:start position:19%
boundary?
When can data be reidentified? Who

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:42.240 align:start position:19%
authorizes that? What must always be
logged and auditable?

00:27:38.799 --> 00:27:46.000 align:start position:19%
What obligations follow the data
downstream

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:52.240 align:start position:19%
and what rights and explanations must
must patients receive and then you know

00:27:49.039 --> 00:27:57.840 align:start position:19%
crucially how will the rules evolve as
technology changes technology and

00:27:55.360 --> 00:28:05.679 align:start position:19%
reidentification capabilities will
change and are changing. So I would say

00:28:01.760 --> 00:28:11.120 align:start position:19%
to consider mandatory periodic review
um you know on a cadence that's up to

00:28:08.240 --> 00:28:15.679 align:start position:19%
you guys exact cadence for the state to
decide but there also maybe should be a

00:28:13.919 --> 00:28:20.880 align:start position:19%
mechanism for interim guidance to be
issued when technology might be moving

00:28:17.520 --> 00:28:26.640 align:start position:19%
faster than the rule cycle. Again, you
know, SOCK or ISO style standards can be

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:30.799 align:start position:19%
useful baselines, but New Mexico, I
think, should go beyond checkbox

00:28:28.399 --> 00:28:39.679 align:start position:19%
compliance and define program specific
privacy and data use boundaries.

00:28:35.520 --> 00:28:46.720 align:start position:19%
And I'd like to leave you with this very
simple slide with these three things.

00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:54.000 align:start position:19%
These three objectives don't have to be
in conflict. Protect the person, protect

00:28:50.159 --> 00:28:58.640 align:start position:19%
the data, preserve the value. Now, New
Mexico does not have to choose between

00:28:56.240 --> 00:29:02.799 align:start position:19%
patient privacy and discovery. We don't
have to choose between trust and

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:07.760 align:start position:19%
innovation. If the rules are built
thoughtfully from the beginning, each

00:29:05.360 --> 00:29:11.840 align:start position:19%
can strengthen the other. Good
governance is what makes responsible

00:29:09.919 --> 00:29:16.640 align:start position:19%
innovation possible because it gives
patients the confidence to participate.

00:29:14.559 --> 00:29:20.240 align:start position:19%
It gives responsible organizations clear
boundaries and it gives researchers

00:29:18.240 --> 00:29:25.039 align:start position:19%
access to useful data without
unnecessarily exposing people. Now today

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:29.360 align:start position:19%
I've deliberately stayed at the
principal level. There's a a much deeper

00:29:27.279 --> 00:29:34.480 align:start position:19%
layer underneath each of these topics
like disclosure risk risk testing

00:29:31.919 --> 00:29:38.960 align:start position:19%
reidentification controls audit models
research environments

00:29:36.399 --> 00:29:45.760 align:start position:19%
uh vendor requirements you know detailed
policy language. Um, but we're not going

00:29:42.640 --> 00:29:51.200 align:start position:19%
to get to that in in 20 minutes. Um, I
would be happy to help work through

00:29:47.840 --> 00:29:57.440 align:start position:19%
those as the program develops.
Thank you guys. Any uh questions? I

00:29:53.520 --> 00:30:00.399 align:start position:19%
think we have about five minutes left.
